{
 "summary": "The formal statements prove three things about R_k(3), defined as the least n such that every k-colouring of E(K_n) has a monochromatic triangle. First, R_k(3)^{1/k} → ∞. Second, for every ε>0 and all sufficiently large k, (1/3−ε)k log k ≤ log R_k(3) ≤ (1+ε)k log k. Third, log R_k(3) = Θ(k log k). The most important caveat is that `erdos_problem_183_explicit`, which is meant to capture the explicit bound of Theorem 1.1 for all k ≥ 2, carries the false hypothesis (1:ℕ)=0, so it is vacuous. The non-asymptotic Theorem 1.1 is therefore not actually formalized.",
 "glossary": [
  {"decl": "TriangleFree", "line": 8, "plain": "A k-colouring C of the edges of K_n has no monochromatic triangle: each colour class is K_3-free.", "standard_concept": "triangle-free k-colouring of K_n", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "ForcesMonochromaticTriangle", "line": 12, "plain": "Every k-colouring of E(K_n) contains a monochromatic triangle.", "standard_concept": "n → (3)_k arrow relation", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "For k=0 and n≥2 no colourings exist, so the statement holds vacuously. This only affects k=0."},
  {"decl": "triangleRamseyNumber", "line": 15, "plain": "R_k(3) = min{n : every k-colouring of E(K_n) has a monochromatic triangle}, defined via sInf.", "standard_concept": "multicolour Ramsey number R(3,...,3)", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "The set is nonempty for every k by Ramsey's theorem, so the sInf ∅ = 0 junk value never occurs. The degenerate value R_0 = 2 is irrelevant asymptotically."}
 ],
 "lean_claims": [
  {"theorem": "erdos_183", "line": 18, "plain": "R_k(3)^{1/k} → ∞ as k → ∞ (over ℕ, real rpow)."},
  {"theorem": "erdos_problem_183_explicit", "line": 25, "plain": "Assuming 1 = 0 in ℕ (false), two things hold: for all k ≥ 2, ((1/(6e^{38}))·k^{1/3}/log k)^k ≤ R_k(3); and R_k(3)^{1/k} → ∞. Because the hypothesis is false, this is vacuous."},
  {"theorem": "triangleRamseyNumber_log_sharp_coefficients", "line": 36, "plain": "For every ε > 0 and all sufficiently large k: (1/3−ε)·k·log k ≤ log R_k(3) ≤ (1+ε)·k·log k."},
  {"theorem": "triangleRamseyNumber_log_isTheta", "line": 45, "plain": "log R_k(3) = Θ(k log k) as k → ∞."}
 ],
 "paper_claims": [
  {"ref": "Theorem 1.1 (3)", "plain": "There is an absolute c > 0 with R_k(3) ≥ (c k^{1/3}/log k)^k for every integer k ≥ 2."},
  {"ref": "Theorem 1.1 (asymptotic)", "plain": "k^{(1/3−o(1))k} ≤ R_k(3) ≤ k^{(1+o(1))k}, and R_k(3) = k^{Θ(k)}."},
  {"ref": "Theorem 1.1 (4)", "plain": "lim R_k(3)^{1/k} = +∞."}
 ],
 "coverage": [
  {"paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1 (3)", "lean_theorem": "erdos_problem_183_explicit", "relation": "not_formalized", "why": "The Lean statement uses an explicit c = 1/(6e^{38}), which would be stronger than the paper. However, the false hypothesis 1 = 0 makes the statement vacuous. The sharp-coefficient theorem is only an eventual, asymptotic consequence of (3), not (3) itself."},
  {"paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1 (asymptotic)", "lean_theorem": "triangleRamseyNumber_log_sharp_coefficients", "relation": "equivalent", "why": "Taking logarithms, this is exactly k^{(1/3−o(1))k} ≤ R_k ≤ k^{(1+o(1))k}."},
  {"paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1 (asymptotic)", "lean_theorem": "triangleRamseyNumber_log_isTheta", "relation": "equivalent", "why": "R_k = k^{Θ(k)} is the same as log R_k = Θ(k log k)."},
  {"paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1 (4)", "lean_theorem": "erdos_183", "relation": "equivalent", "why": "Direct statement of R_k^{1/k} → ∞."}
 ],
